  "summary": "A distinctive beading style characterized by colorful floral patterns and geometric designs on clothing and accessories.",
  "description": "Lakota beadwork features vibrant floral motifs and geometric patterns, evolving from earlier quillwork techniques through European trade bead introduction. This tradition represents cultural resilience and adaptation, maintaining aesthetic continuity while incorporating new materials.",
  "culturalContext": "Porcupine quillwork is an art form unique to North America. Before the introduction of glass beads, quillwork was a major decorative element used by the peoples who resided in the porcupine's natural habitat, which included indigenous peoples of the Subarctic, Northeastern Woodlands, and Northern Plains. The use of quills in designs spans from Maine to Alaska. Quillworking tools were discovered in Alberta, Canada and date back to the 6th century CE.",
